diff --git a/README.md b/README.md
index f2fa36bc15d05ed30fcda551988b13398f3d10ba..94e0eabd1b6e9c1caa00b608c6377ead16719cf2 100644
--- a/README.md
+++ b/README.md
@@ -309,11 +309,18 @@ storage. Paths must be canonical, unique, and cannot cover init, `/`, `/proc`,
 Without assignments, native image access remains read-only.
 
 This backend supports regular-file open/create/exclusive-create/truncate/append,
-read/write/pread, ftruncate, seek, close, basic descriptor flags, mkdir, file
-unlink, chmod,
-and stat/fstat/fstatat/statx metadata. Relative paths use the configured workdir;
-directory descriptors, directory enumeration/removal, owned symlinks, hardlinks,
-rename, file mappings and execution of generated files are unsupported. Component-wise image-link traversal preserves assignment
+read/write/pread, ftruncate, seek, close, descriptor flags, mkdir/unlink/rmdir,
+owned directory descriptions/getdents64, rename/renameat/renameat2 with NOREPLACE,
+chmod and stat/fstat/fstatat/statx metadata. Access and readlink probes stay in
+the owned namespace; regular files accept O_NOCTTY. Directory cookies and inode
+identity follow open descriptions across aliases, fork, rename and removal.
+Namespace changes validate types, capacity and replacement before mutation.
+Relative AT_FDCWD paths follow the calling process's image cwd; owned directory
+FDs resolve relative operations through their current VFS identity. Image chdir,
+getcwd and relative image exec retain native per-process cwd semantics. Entering
+owned directories as cwd, relative image-directory FD access, owned symlinks,
+hardlinks, mappings and generated-file execution remain unsupported.
+Component-wise image-link traversal preserves assignment
 precedence, missing components and non-directory errors. Native image operations
 use a temporary canonical path below the stopped workload's stack; an unavailable
 scratch area or overlapping output buffer returns EFAULT. This is a bounded
@@ -342,8 +349,8 @@ lower operator limits. Allocation respects that lower soft limit; runtime
 NOFILE-limit changes are currently denied. Reported limits are not inflated to
 accommodate backend handle ranges. Fork copies public descriptor tables while
 sharing open descriptions and owned-object lifetimes. Successful exec closes
-CLOEXEC entries; failed exec preserves the table. Relative directory-fd access
-and descriptor polling remain unsupported.
+CLOEXEC entries; failed exec preserves the table. Owned directory-fd operations
+are supported; descriptor polling remains unsupported.
 Guest-memory copies respect page protections, including partial read copyout.
 No writable host descriptor is exposed. Data disappears at domain teardown;
 there is no retention, export, storage generation or separate storage-quota setting.
